s maintain moderate lengths of 35–58 pages on average, while guidebooks extend to approximately 78 pages. Brochures and administrative files remain relatively compact, whereas financial reports again emerge as the longest category.\n\nCollectively, these two benchmarks provide comprehensive coverage ranging from brief news articles to extensive technical and financial documentation. This establishes diverse and challenging evaluation contexts for multimodal document understanding tasks.\n\nA.2 ADDITIONAL CASE STUDIES\n\n• Demonstrating Visual Reasoning Capabilities. Figure 5 illustrates how RAG-Anything handles complex visual reasoning tasks involving chart interpretation. The query asks which GCAN submodel component removal yields the lowest accuracy on Twitter15. Traditional approaches struggle with spatial relationships between visual elements. RAG-Anything addresses this challenge by constructing a structured graph representation of the bar plot. Bars, axis labels, and legends"}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > 3.2 PERFORMANCE COMPARISON\n- Neighbor Text: 54.6% for 101–200 pages; 68.8% vs. 55.0% for 200+ pages). On MMLongBench, RAG-Anything demonstrates consistent improvements across all length categories, achieving accuracy gains of 3.4 points for 11–50 pages, 9.3 points for 51–100 pages, and 7.9 points for 101–200 pages. These findings confirm that our dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism is particularly effective for long-document reasoning tasks. 3.3 ARCHITECTURAL VALIDATION WITH ABLATION STUDIES\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/dacf546a791ecdbbb43fad0b51f885e5ea1b8f916706749870a0a8ab7cf4da63.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 2: Performance evaluation across documents of varying lengths.\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a vertical bar chart titled 'Figure 2: Performance evaluation across documents of varying lengths.' The chart visualizes the distribution of QA pairs across different document length categories. The x-axis is labeled 'Page Range' and is divided into five bins: '1-10', '11-50', '51-100', '101-200', and '200+'. The y-axis is labeled 'QA Pair Count' and ranges from 0 to 700 in increments of 100. The bars are colored light blue. The bar for the '11-50' page range is the tallest, reaching approximately 700. The bar for '51-100' pages is the second tallest, at approximately 150. The bar for '101-200' pages is around 100. The bars for '1-10' and '200+' pages are very short, indicating a low count of QA pairs. The chart shows that the majority of QA pairs are concentrated in documents of 11-50 pages, with significantly fewer QA pairs in shorter or longer documents. This figure relates to the surrounding text which discusses performance comparison across varying document len